Fixes issue dotnet/runtime#57472

Main pr dotnet/runtime#57479

Also backported to 5.0 in dotnet/runtime#57562 and 3.1 in #43102. This PR will pave the way for backporting it to the `System.Memory` OOB package for .NET Framework.

# Customer Impact

A customer has encountered dotnet/runtime#57472 while on .NET Framework and reported it in dotnet/runtime#67295, necessiating backporting it once again. As an example provided in that issue, this memory manager:

``` csharp
private sealed class BasicMemoryManager : MemoryManager<byte>
{
    private readonly byte[] _buffer;
    public BasicMemoryManager(int size = 1024) => _buffer = new byte[size];

    protected override bool TryGetArray(out ArraySegment<byte> segment)
    {
        segment = new ArraySegment<byte>(_buffer);
        return true;
    }
    public override Span<byte> GetSpan() => _buffer;

    public override MemoryHandle Pin(int elementIndex = 0) => throw new NotSupportedException();
    public override void Unpin() => throw new NotSupportedException();
    protected override void Dispose(bool disposing) { }
}
```

will fail two of the following tests:

``` csharp
[Theory]
[InlineData(0, 32)]
[InlineData(0, 29)]
[InlineData(32, 32)] // fail: length 0
[InlineData(32, 29)] // fail: length -3
public void OffsetTest(int offset, int length)
{
    var mgr = new BasicMemoryManager(1024);
    var slice = mgr.Memory.Slice(offset, length);
    Assert.Equal(length, slice.Length);

    var ros = new ReadOnlySequence<byte>(slice);
    Assert.True(ros.IsSingleSegment);
    Assert.Equal(length, ros.Length);
}
```

# Testing

Failing test has been added in this PR.

# Risk

Low, as the change is very simple and obvious when you look at `ReadOnlySequence<T>` ctor that accepts `ReadOnlyMemory<T>` (notice line 133 versus lines 140 and 148):

https://github.com/dotnet/corefx/blob/0d40b482e6b6c232131dc808182d6b32d9d37394/src/System.Memory/src/System/Buffers/ReadOnlySequence.cs#L130-L149

Moreover, the changed code gets triggered only when creating a `ReadOnlySequence` from a `ReadOnlyMemory` backed by a `MemoryManager`. All other use cases (including creating from an array or from a `ReadOnlyMemory` backed by an array or a string) are unaffected. And the changes are identical to those in the other backports.

# Regression

This is not a regression.

# REVIEW ONLY — DO NOT MERGE

This draft pull request is a combined review view of the browser-tools stack plus an **experimental provider-only simplification**. **It is not an authoritative merge unit and must not be merged.** Do not enable auto-merge.

The authoritative merge stack remains, in order:

1. [#56071 — Gateway-native WASM hot reload](https://github.com/dotnet/sdk/pull/56071): hosts standalone Blazor WebAssembly browser tooling in the `dotnet watch` Gateway provider, including replay and launch-selection coverage.
2. [#56072 — MVC/Razor Pages TagHelper activation](https://github.com/dotnet/sdk/pull/56072): activates supported MVC and Razor Pages responses through the body TagHelper pipeline.
3. [#56080 — Unified modern browser-tools forwarding](https://github.com/dotnet/sdk/pull/56080): forwards server-hosted HTTP/WebSocket traffic to the watch-owned provider and limits activation to `dotnet watch` builds.

The tree through commit `73667f3d1f086479f5d8babb52c7b3ed015f1253` is identical to the final authoritative stack head. This review branch then adds experimental provider-only and minimization commits; its current tree is intentionally **not** the authoritative stack tree.

The experimental layer:

- uses one hosting-startup forwarder for supported application hosts, including standalone WebAssembly development servers;
- retains and replays WebAssembly updates in the provider;
- removes legacy response rewriting, application-hosted browser scripts/endpoints, and transition-only launch abstractions;
- activates MVC and Razor Pages through the body TagHelper component;
- activates Blazor static SSR and Interactive Server through a build-only Web SDK `afterWebStarted` initializer;
- activates WebAssembly across supported TFMs through a build-only Wasm SDK `onRuntimeReady` initializer, using the agent API on .NET 10+ and the existing runtime apply API on older TFMs;
- preserves the existing browser client behavior and UI while adding provider discovery, replay ordering, generation checks, reconnection, and duplicate suppression.

The minimization review reduced the aggregate diff from 74 files / +2673 / −3978 to **64 files / +2134 / −3636** by removing unused interfaces, options, configurators, and callback seams; reverting unrelated changes; preserving existing names and call shapes; and keeping the browser client as a recognizable directory move rather than a rename/rewrite.

## Validation

Validated exact head `7c49792a19c59d4d8a8681f0238fbcc4deb5b8ac`:

- full `build.cmd`: passed;
- focused browser-tools, Web/WASM static-asset, and `dotnet-watch` tests: passed;
- generated compatibility applications: **34/34 restore and build** with the branch SDK (`11.0.100-dev`);
- standalone WebAssembly gate across net8/net9/net10/net11: **4/4 passed**;
- clean Playwright CLI browser matrix (`--workers=1`): **34/34 passed**;
- main HTTP responses: 34/34 successful; console/page-error cases: 0;
- every case reported `remainingPids: []`; post-run matching processes: 0.

The matrix covers MVC, Razor Pages, standalone Blazor WebAssembly, Blazor static SSR, Interactive WebAssembly, Interactive Server, legacy Blazor Server, and six mixed hosted-WASM server/client TFM pairs across supported .NET 8–11 combinations.

Please use this PR only to review the aggregate design and experimental simplification. Do not merge it.
